WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.210
- [Reporter] Canceled and delayed flights,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.110
long lines and packed planes

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.350
have become the new normal for air travel,

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.390
which is worrying some
travelers this holiday season.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.329
- [Woman] I have been in
line for almost an hour

00:00:12.329 --> 00:00:13.860
and have not moved.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.140
- [Reporter] In early October,

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.770
Southwest canceled more than 2,000 flights

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.520
over the span of three days
due to scheduling problems.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.680
Later that month, American
Airlines said staffing shortages

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.390
contributed to the more
than 1,900 flights scrubbed

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.910
during Halloween weekend.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.200
- We've seen generally
there's more delays,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.360
more canceled flights

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.488
and several really bad episodes

00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:36.789
where there were kind of
cascades of cancellations

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:40.360
that resulted in 1,000s
of flights being canceled

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.870
over a couple of day periods.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.430
- [Reporter] So what exactly is driving

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.030
all of these airline disruptions?

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.190
And what problems will passengers face

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.893
as we head towards the holidays?

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.130
- So air travel really was
pretty much dormant last year

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.068
due to the pandemic,

00:00:54.068 --> 00:00:55.890
hardly anybody was traveling

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.170
and then that changed really
dramatically this past spring.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.150
- [Reporter] Like many industries

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.880
struggling with an increasingly
tight labor market,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.140
airlines are facing staffing shortages.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.220
- While the airlines got a lot
of government money last year

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.150
to keep all their employees working

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.680
or on the payroll at least,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.481
they really just had not enough

00:01:14.481 --> 00:01:17.540
for all of those employees to
do and they were concerned,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.040
how long is the pandemic gonna last.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.400
So they encouraged or really
urged a lot of employees

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.330
who were close to
retirement to retire early

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.100
or they asked people to take
long-term leaves of absence,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.720
sometimes for a couple of years.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.300
- [Reporter] Now, airlines are
racing to bring back workers

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.150
and hire pilots, flight
attendants and ground staff.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.210
But hiring has been difficult as companies

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.820
face fiercer competition
for certain roles.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.510
- You know, they are sort
of seeing some strains

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.950
in some of these positions,

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.320
like ground workers and ramp
workers and customer service

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.880
and all the other 1,000s of people

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.350
that are needed to make an airline run.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.010
But suddenly a lot of
those potential employees

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.260
have a lot of other options.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.720
- [Reporter] Meanwhile, the
added burden of extra shifts

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.062
and longer hours for the
workers companies do have,

00:01:59.062 --> 00:02:03.190
can lead to higher rates of
staff burnout and sick leave.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.262
- So, for the employees who are there,

00:02:04.262 --> 00:02:08.380
some of them are complaining
about new levels of strain,

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.020
especially over the summer
and the last couple of months.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.530
Airlines like at Southwest,

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.050
have been saying that
they're having problems

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.730
with higher rates of sick
leave and absenteeism.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.897
And some of that might be due to COVID.

00:02:18.897 --> 00:02:20.760
- [Reporter] As a result, worker unions

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.070
have become more vocal about another issue

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.350
disrupting air travel:

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.040
Scheduling mismanagement.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.540
Typical hiccups that may have
normally delayed a flight

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.560
for one day,

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.400
turn into multi-day meltdowns

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.550
caused by staff scheduling holes.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.430
Consider this domino effect:

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.150
over optimistic flight scheduling

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:38.650
in the midst of a staffing shortage

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.770
may lead to employees being overworked,

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.770
creating higher levels of
staff burnout and sick leave

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.230
and leading to even more scheduling issues

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.400
and canceled flights.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.826
- The pilots have said,

00:02:48.826 --> 00:02:53.060
maybe they're a little bit
reluctant to pick up extra trips

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.700
when there's an option
to pick up some overtime,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.620
because if you thought
you'd be willing to work

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:57.453
a three-day trip,

00:02:57.453 --> 00:02:59.950
but if it turns into a
six day trip unexpectedly,

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.260
you're going to have a problem.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.506
- What we want to see, is
more efficient scheduling

00:03:04.506 --> 00:03:08.210
that goes on during these
irregular operations.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.100
- [Reporter] In a November
13th memo to flight attendants,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.570
Southwest Airlines said it
was adding more reserve crews

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.380
to support the reliability
of its scheduling.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.120
Of course, external
factors like bad weather

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.400
or technical issues play a role
in airline disruptions too.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.640
But now, these issues are
compounded by staffing shortages

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.310
and scheduling issues,
creating chain reactions

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.390
that can leave thousands
of passengers stranded.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.880
- Americans that have
got knocked off course

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.170
by a couple of days of
really high winds in Dallas.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.560
And that resulted in traffic
management problems in Dallas,

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.480
that led to fewer flights being
able to take off from there.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.304
And that kind of backed
up their whole system.

00:03:46.304 --> 00:03:47.470
- [Reporter] American Airlines says

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.280
it is proactively canceling flights

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.100
to provide more scheduling
certainty for crews.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.170
- Airlines are trying to do things

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.260
to make sure that people
do keep showing up to work

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.480
and they have the staffing they need.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.070
- [Reporter] Airlines
are expecting a surge

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.080
in holiday bookings amid
declining COVID-19 cases

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.800
and rising vaccination rates.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.470
But fuller planes and more
robust flight schedules

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.460
often mean more difficulties
when things go wrong.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.510
To avoid staffing pitfalls
and scheduling challenges,

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.620
some airlines have scaled
back flight options.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:18.920
- That's something we've
seen with Southwest.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.490
It just has fewer flights than it used to.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.970
So when it does have something go wrong

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.900
and it does need to put
people on other flights

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.300
to try and help them get
where they needed to go,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.360
if flights are full, that can
be more difficult than that.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.240
There are just fewer recovery options.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.530
- [Reporter] Something else to consider

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.450
when preparing for holiday travel:

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.980
smaller airports may be overwhelmed

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.880
by an increase in travelers.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.750
- So there's been a lot more
demands for leisure travel

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.370
to places in Florida,

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:45.330
depending on the time of year,

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.420
to ski destinations or places
out west or national parks.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.040
And that just means some of these airports

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.030
that had historically been pretty small

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.840
or maybe not had huge traffic numbers,

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.280
are now just seeing an
influx of new airlines

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.990
adding service, more
flights, more passengers.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.450
- [Reporter] Meanwhile,
more crowds can also mean

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.723
more unruly passengers.

00:05:03.723 --> 00:05:05.980
(passengers fighting)

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.250
The Federal Aviation
Administration reported

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.830
over 5,000 passenger
disputes so far this year.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.860
So travelers flying this holiday season

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.510
are reminded to pack some patience.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.490
- Everyone's kind of going
through it together a little bit,

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.323
I guess.

00:05:19.323 --> 00:05:20.700
I think we're all just going
to have to kind of learn

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.200
how to do this again.

